# The age public keys that local/seal-signing-backup encrypts the signing
# material to. Public keys only, so this file is not itself a secret —
# but it lives with the archive, in the private dotfiles repo:
#
#   ~/code/dotfiles/secrets/recipients.txt
#
# There have to be at least two, and the second one is the point of the
# whole exercise: a backup of this laptop that only a key on this laptop
# can open goes wherever the laptop goes.
#
# The first is this machine. Make it once, if there is none yet:
#
#   mkdir -p ~/.config/age && age-keygen -o ~/.config/age/identity.txt
#   chmod 600 ~/.config/age/identity.txt
#
# age-keygen prints the public key as it writes the private one; it is
# also in a comment at the top of the file, and `age-keygen -y
# ~/.config/age/identity.txt` prints it again.
age1exampleexampleexampleexampleexampleexampleexampleexamplev7l0e0

# The second is kept off this machine, and how is a decision rather than
# a command. Some options, roughly in order of how much they ask of you:
#
#   - A key generated on another computer you own, with its identity file
#     staying there.
#   - A key generated on this one, written down or printed, and the
#     identity file then deleted from disk. age keys are one short line.
#   - A YubiKey, through age-plugin-yubikey, which gives a recipient line
#     like `age1yubikey1...`.
#
# What none of them can be is a second key sitting next to the first.
age1exampleexampleexampleexampleexampleexampleexampleexample5j2k9q

# An SSH public key works as a recipient too, and `age -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519`
# opens it. Convenient, but the private key is usually on this machine as
# well, so it does not count as the second recipient.
#
#   ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3Nz... you@example.com
